00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.850
So first, what's our motivation?

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:11.217
What are we trying to solve as
part of the Cloud AI platform?

00:02:11.217 --> 00:02:13.050
The key problem that
we're trying to address

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.450
is the fact that
building and deploying

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.020
real-life ML applications
is harder and costlier

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.490
than it needs to be.

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:22.350
And that is because
of lack of tooling

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.560
that covers the end-to-end
machine learning life cycle,

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.470
the end-to-end life cycle for
development and deployment

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.480
of custom machine
learning applications.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.340
And that is the key problem
that we're trying to solve.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.630
Now the next couple
of slides will

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.450
be a little bit of
repetition for folks that

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.190
attended the previous webinar.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.920
But nevertheless, I will cover
this content again real quick

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.550
for those that may watch this
presentation later on YouTube.

00:02:55.686 --> 00:02:57.060
When we spoke to
customers, there

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.620
were three high
level themes that

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:03.180
emerged as persistent problems
that hold customers back

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:06.310
from building successful
machine learning applications.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.490
The first problem was
that of deployment.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.807
Today's infrastructure is
brittle and opinionated,

00:03:10.807 --> 00:03:13.140
and the environments change
when you go from your laptop

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.160
to the cluster.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.070
And also in today's world,
hybrid is a reality.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.350
The other big problem
is that of talent.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:22.810
It's just really hard to find
machine learning experts.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.630
And if every machine
learning application

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.670
needs a team of
experts, well that

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.500
is going to be a
severe bottleneck

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.050
for the successful deployment
of machine learning.

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:33.330
Then the last piece
was collaboration.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.100
Everyone is
reinventing the wheel

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:39.270
and re-implementing components,
whereas the ideal state

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:42.120
of the world is where different
teams collaborate and reuse

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.390
assets that they're building.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:47.320
These three problems
at a high level

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.140
are the problems
we want to tackle.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.620
And the three pillars
towards our solution

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:57.350
are Kubeflow, reusable
pipelines, and AI Hub.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.060
Kubeflow is a popular
open source project,

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.790
and Kubeflow aims to simplify
the end-to-end machine learning

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:03.660
life cycle.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.930
It provides a set of
services and libraries

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.090
that are seamlessly
integrated, that address

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.820
the needs of the various
stages of a machine learning

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:12.960
application.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.480
Kubeflow is a
collection of services

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.910
that runs on the Kubernetes
execution engine.

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.940
And we chose Kubernetes because
Kubernetes is effectively

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.310
the de facto for deploying
applications that

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:25.620
leverage a cluster of machines.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.270
So by leveraging
Kubernetes, we are

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.060
able to effectively
provide a hybrid solution,

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.280
because Kubernetes is
the de facto platform

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.470
on premise as well as
on multiple clouds.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:43.200
Kubeflow enables customers
to move their applications

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:47.310
from on premise to the
clouds and so forth.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:50.010
A couple more key
aspects of Kubeflow,

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:53.340
it is an open
source library that

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.530
supports multiple machine
learning frameworks.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:57.000
It is not limited to TensorFlow.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.480
It supports scikit-learn,
XGBoost, Python,

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.550
and multiple other frameworks.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:04.950
And because it's open source,
we work with the community

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:08.850
to assimilate newer frameworks
that become popular.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.100
One more key thing to
highlight is the fact that,

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:15.070
while Kubeflow is open source,
if you deploy Kubeflow on GCP--

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:19.560
particularly on Google's
Kubernetes Engine GKE--

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.630
we have built some
integrations that

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:24.240
make it very seamless
to deploy Kubeflow.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.420
And also, for the most
popular Kubeflow services,

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.020
you can leverage fully managed
equivalents on Google Cloud.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:35.400
So you don't always have to
manage your infrastructure.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:37.580
The other aspect is
reusable pipelines.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:40.730
And over here, the Lego
analogy is very apt.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:44.270
We want to get to a world where
components and pipelines built

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:46.560
by others can
seamlessly be reused.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.500
We don't want people to
reinvent the wheel all the time.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:51.200
And this is
particularly important

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.680
because in today's world, every
machine learning application

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.492
is considered to be a
bespoke application.

00:05:57.492 --> 00:05:59.450
So whenever someone's
building a custom machine

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:01.408
learning application,
the first thing they need

00:06:01.408 --> 00:06:03.590
is a data scientist or a
machine learning engineer

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:05.370
that is focused on
that application

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.400
and will build a
bespoke solution.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.050
But that is not always the case.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.090
Often, what you need
to do is actually look

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:12.710
at other successful
applications,

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:14.390
and reuse the stuff
they've built,

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:16.640
and tweak the components
that you need to tweak.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.390
And that is what
we want to enable.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:22.210
And then the third
aspect is the AI Hub.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:27.010
You can think it as a one stop
shop, or a one stop catalog,

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:29.460
for machine learning content.

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.120
And I use the word
content because we

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.070
will have multiple
different types of assets.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:36.190
We will clearly have pipelines
built with Kubeflow Pipelines

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:37.030
infrastructure.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:38.830
We'll also have
usable components

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:41.430
that you can leverage as a
part of Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:44.860
But we'll also have other
assets like pre train models,

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:47.680
or embeddings, or
even just notebooks

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.960
where a particular
model is being trained.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:53.050
So long story short, AI Hub
becomes your one stop shop

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:54.730
to find assets
that you can either

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:57.970
reuse, or use as inspiration
to build your own custom

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:00.261
applications.

00:07:00.261 --> 00:07:00.760
All right.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:02.710
So with that, now
let's do a deeper dive

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:05.410
into the specific
problems that inspired

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:07.874
the Kubeflow Pipelines project.

00:07:07.874 --> 00:07:11.000
So the key premise here is
the fact that in addition

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.940
to the actual machine learning--

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:15.740
What I mean by the
actual ML, we're

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:18.050
talking about the code
that trains the model

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:20.160
or does influence
with the model.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.660
In addition to that, there
are multiple other phases

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:25.970
that you actually have
to implement and manage

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:29.170
for a successful end-to-end
machine learning application.

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:31.760
There's data integration,
data collection,

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:35.000
feature engineering, data
validation, model deployment,

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.820
and then tooling to capture
metrics and orchestrate

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:39.480
all these pieces together.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.180
And that can often
get non-trivial.

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:44.840
And that is a core problem
that we want to solve.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:47.660
Let me also present a
few customer quotes that

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.510
will make things more tangible.

00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:53.180
One customer that we spoke to
said that they don't really

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:54.710
have an ML workflow today.

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:56.900
What they've basically
done is taken workflows

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:59.600
that they've used for
general software development

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.760
and applied that in the
machine learning context.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:04.660
But that lacks the
things that ML needs,

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:06.740
and that is one problem
we want to solve.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.930
The other problem is
we spoke to customers

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:12.260
where they don't retrain their
models on a frequent basis,

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:14.750
despite the fact that model
freshness was actually

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:17.060
important for their business.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:20.000
And part of the reason they
don't retrain models as often

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.922
is because the retraining
process was very manual.

00:08:22.922 --> 00:08:24.380
But we wanted to
solve that problem

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:26.180
and make it very
easy for customers

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:29.780
to set up constant or retraining
pipelines that can be scheduled

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:32.809
to run on a periodic basis
so that they can get newer,

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:35.830
updated models more easily.

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:37.370
A third customer
that we spoke to

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:39.770
basically said ML
experimentation

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:42.570
is like the Wild West today.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:45.020
Everyone is using ad-hoc tools.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.120
There is no standardization.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:49.070
It's very hard to go
back and understand

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:51.290
what led to the production
of a particular model.

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:53.610
It's hard to know what
techniques have been tried,

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:56.177
what experiments have been
run, and so on and so forth.

00:08:56.177 --> 00:08:57.260
And we want to solve that.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:58.884
We want to make it
easy for you to have

00:08:58.884 --> 00:09:01.250
one place where you can track
all the experiments that

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:03.830
were done.

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:08.170
So with that, let me dive
into the demo section

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:11.290
where we cover pertinent aspects
of the Kubeflow Pipelines

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:13.180
project.

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:17.560
Before we dive in, I want to
talk about the three feature

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:20.110
buckets into which the
capabilities of Kubeflow

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:22.830
Pipelines can be classified.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:24.730
The three buckets
are machine learning

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:28.780
workflow orchestration, rapid
reliable experimentation,

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:31.360
and sharing, reuse,
and composability.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:33.730
So the Kubeflow Pipelines
project, at a very high level,

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:36.520
is solving problems that fall
under these three buckets.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:40.641
So first, let me talk about
ML workflow orchestration.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:46.410
Because we've been talking about
multi-step machine learning

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:49.560
pipelines, let me make
things more concrete by first

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:52.980
presenting a visual
depiction of a pipeline that

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:56.050
has been executed on the
Kubeflow Pipelines framework.

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:58.740
So this is the Kubeflow
Pipelines console

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:01.220
depicting an actual
pipeline that was executed.

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:03.540
Now in this particular
case, this pipeline

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.270
starts with a validation
step where incoming data

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:09.780
is analyzed to make sure that
there are no data quality

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:10.560
issues.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:12.570
After that, there is
a pre-processing step,

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.580
followed by a training step.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:17.070
And then the model that's
produced in the training step,

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:20.580
in one scenario, is used
for doing prediction

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:22.110
on offline data.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:24.060
In another scenario,
the same model

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.580
is being deployed to
a serving endpoint.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:28.020
And then, there's
a third process

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:32.220
where the model is being
used to do deeper analysis

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.670
on the validation data set.

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:36.970
Now, this is just an
illustrative pipeline.

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:39.960
And you can build multiple
different configurations

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:43.050
of pipelines with
this framework.

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:45.630
A few key things to
highlight here are,

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:48.570
in addition to the visual
depiction of the stages

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:51.270
of the pipeline-- which makes
it very easy for you to come

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:55.290
in and understand what
that pipeline is doing--

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:59.280
you actually get a whole set
of rich information required

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:03.450
to understand in sufficient
detail what was happening when

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:05.240
this pipeline was executed.

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:07.350
So you can see all the
configuration parameters

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:08.730
and all the input
parameters that

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:11.610
were provided to the pipeline.

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:13.110
For each step of
the pipeline, you

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:16.200
can drill deep and
select a particular step,

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.480
and then see the
inputs and outputs

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:19.760
of that individual step.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:21.420
So the inputs that
were provided,

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:23.700
what were the outputs
that were produced.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:25.830
You can also look at
logs that were produced,

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:28.890
so that you can debug
things when they fail or get

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:30.720
a deeper understanding
of what happened

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:33.180
when that step was run.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:36.540
What is particularly
compelling is certain steps

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:40.180
will produce interesting
data that can be visualized.

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:43.624
So for example, if
you're doing analysis--

00:11:43.624 --> 00:11:45.540
and in this particular
case, the analysis step

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:48.840
was implemented using the
TensorFlow Model Analysis

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:51.510
component that's part
of the TFX library.

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:53.580
It produces a rich
set of artifacts

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:57.390
that can be visualized with
this slice and dice UI.

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:59.910
And the visualization
of the metrics

00:11:59.910 --> 00:12:02.110
is embedded right
here in the console.

00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:04.050
So for the metrics
that are produced,

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:05.620
you can visualize
them right here.

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:07.078
So in this particular
case, you can

00:12:07.078 --> 00:12:11.470
do slicing and dicing of the
metrics produced by that model.

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:13.000
Let's take another example.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:15.300
If I go to the
prediction step, I

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.750
can visualize the output,
which is tabular output where

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:22.740
each offline data set and the
corresponding score assigned

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:25.290
to it can be visualized.

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:27.690
And if I go to the
training step, for example,

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:32.432
I can, by the click of a button,
open the TensorBoard instance,

00:12:32.432 --> 00:12:34.890
where I can now get a deeper
understanding of what happened

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:37.700
when that model was trained.

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:41.950
So long story short, a lot
of rich visual information

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:44.020
is readily available
at your fingertips

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:45.880
so that you can
understand what happened

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:49.310
when a pipeline was done.

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:52.690
So let me go back and cover
some pertinent details

00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:56.100
about what
constitutes a pipeline

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:58.810
and exactly what
are the pieces that

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:01.720
define a Kubeflow Pipeline.

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:04.600
In its simplest form,
a Kubeflow Pipeline

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:09.490
is a set of containerized
machine learning tasks.

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:11.170
The reason we
chose containers is

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:13.060
because containers
are the industry

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:15.280
standard for portability,
repeatability,

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:16.370
and encapsulation.

00:13:16.370 --> 00:13:20.350
And of course, we want you to go
from prototyping to production

00:13:20.350 --> 00:13:22.960
as quickly as possible,
and thus containerizing

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:24.130
your steps enables that.

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:26.830
We also wanted repeatability
and reliability,

00:13:26.830 --> 00:13:29.340
and again, containers are the
industry standard solution

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:29.840
for that.

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:32.040
And that's why we did
not want to reinvent that

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:33.830
and re-leverage containers.

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:37.480
But that being said, even
though each step of the pipeline

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:40.450
is a containerized task,
that doesn't mean it

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:42.460
has to run on a single node.

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:45.970
A given task can run
in distributed fashion.

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:49.240
It can be a model that's
trained in distributed fashion.

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:52.690
It can be an Apache Spark step
which is doing distributed data

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:55.390
processing or an Apache Beam
step that's doing distributed

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:56.716
data processing.

00:13:56.716 --> 00:13:58.090
The key thing I
want to highlight

00:13:58.090 --> 00:14:00.340
is even though a given
step is encapsulated

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:02.170
as a container,
when it's actually

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:04.180
deployed on the
Kubernetes cluster,

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:07.540
it can run on
multiple worker nodes.

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:10.190
So it can run in
distributed fashion.

00:14:10.190 --> 00:14:11.842
Another thing worth
highlighting is

00:14:11.842 --> 00:14:13.300
the different steps
of the pipeline

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:14.830
can use different hardware.

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:18.400
So you can have the training
step, which needs GPUs,

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:20.920
and then the pre processing
steps probably just

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:27.352
need a high memory machine
for distributed processing.

00:14:27.352 --> 00:14:29.560
But the key thing is,
different steps of the pipeline

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:31.430
can leverage different
forms of hardware.

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:33.580
And it's very easy
for you to specify

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:36.160
what hardware is required.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:37.540
A third thing I
want to highlight

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:41.320
is the fact that even though
a step of the pipeline

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:45.370
is executed as a container,
these containerized steps

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:48.204
can easily invoke Cloud
native services that

00:14:48.204 --> 00:14:49.120
are running elsewhere.

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:51.280
So for example, you can
have a containerized step

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:54.790
that is calling Cloud
Machine Learning APIs.

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:57.310
Or, you can have a containerized
step that is calling out

00:14:57.310 --> 00:15:01.120
to other fully managed services
like BigQuery, or Cloud Machine

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:04.400
Learning Engine, or Dataflow.

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:07.300
So that's a core part of
what a Kubeflow Pipeline is.

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:10.570
It's a set of containers that
encapsulate machine learning

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:11.630
tasks.

00:15:11.630 --> 00:15:14.950
The other part of it is
specifying the higher level

00:15:14.950 --> 00:15:17.710
workflow, specifying
the sequence in which

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:21.560
these tasks need to be run.

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:24.800
And to do that, we provide
a very intuitive Python

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:27.860
SDK that I'll demo
in a few seconds.

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:30.080
Then the third piece that
constitutes a pipeline

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:32.920
are the set of parameters
that the user has to pass in.

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:35.030
So when we say a
Kubeflow Pipeline,

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:37.040
that essentially
constitutes the containers

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:39.710
for the individual steps,
the higher level workflow

00:15:39.710 --> 00:15:43.560
definition, and the parameters
that the user has to pass in.

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:48.530
So with that, let's go back and
take a look at how a Kubeflow

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:51.380
Pipeline is authored.

00:15:51.380 --> 00:15:54.540
So as I mentioned, we provided
a very intuitive Python SDK.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:56.630
And time and again when
we spoke to our customers,

00:15:56.630 --> 00:15:59.060
everyone said Python
is their lingua franca.

00:15:59.060 --> 00:16:02.510
So essentially, we
decided to provide

00:16:02.510 --> 00:16:06.650
a very Python-ic SDK that will
feel intuitive to our audience.

00:16:06.650 --> 00:16:10.280
And this Python SDK can, for
example, be invoked from an IDE

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:11.450
or even from a notebook.

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:14.000
And because authoring
machine learning components

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:15.950
from notebooks is as
popular these days,

00:16:15.950 --> 00:16:19.610
I'll actually demo usage of
the Kubeflow Pipelines SDK

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:20.884
from a notebook.

00:16:20.884 --> 00:16:22.550
So when it comes to
altering a pipeline,

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:25.170
there are two distinct phases.

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:27.740
One is defining the
higher level workflow,

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:30.440
and then the other is defining
the individual components.

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:33.710
And first, let me start with
the higher level workflow.

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:35.460
And what I mean by the
high level workflow

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:38.160
is the definition of the
sequence in which these steps

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:40.590
need to be executed.

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:43.160
So to define the workflow,
essentially authoring

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:47.350
the workflow is as intuitive
as authoring a Python function.

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:49.280
You essentially define
a Python function that

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:50.490
encapsulates your workflow.

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:53.452
And then you annotate that
with a function decorator

00:16:53.452 --> 00:16:55.910
that essentially signifies that
this is a Kubeflow Pipeline

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:57.200
definition.

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:00.500
For your function, you
will provide arguments,

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:03.080
which are essentially
the inputs that an end

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:05.493
user needs to provide
while running the pipeline.

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:17.780
The arguments can
be annotated so

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:20.210
that you can specify a
name for each argument

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:22.700
and also specify
a default value.

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:25.990
And we'll come back to why that
is very useful and significant.

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:28.190
So long story short, you
define a Python function.

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:29.370
You specify arguments.

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:30.980
These are the inputs
to the function.

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:33.120
And then you start specifying
the different steps

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:35.120
of the pipeline and how
they're hooked together.

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:38.030
Each step of the pipeline is
invoked like a Python function.

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:39.990
At this point, you're
wondering, wait a minute.

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:42.470
Each step of the pipeline
is a containerized step.

00:17:42.470 --> 00:17:44.195
How did it become
a Python function?

00:17:44.195 --> 00:17:46.070
Just hold on to that
thought, and I'll answer

00:17:46.070 --> 00:17:48.540
that question in a few minutes.

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:51.590
But for now, assume that
each step of the pipeline

00:17:51.590 --> 00:17:53.510
is encapsulated as
a Python function.

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:55.280
So to specify the
workflow, it's as

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:57.890
easy as calling the
corresponding function,

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:00.547
providing the inputs that
that function requires.

00:18:00.547 --> 00:18:02.630
And then the sequence in
which the different steps

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:08.180
need to be executed is specified
by connecting the output of one

00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:10.670
function and providing
that as the input

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:12.270
to a downstream function.

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:15.710
So in this particular case,
the first step of the pipeline

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:18.890
produces some output, which
is pre processed output.

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:22.010
And then the pre processed
output is provided as input

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:23.281
to the training step.

00:18:23.281 --> 00:18:25.280
And that's how you specify
the sequence in which

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:27.380
the steps need to be run.

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:30.800
So that was specifying the
workflow orchestration.

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:32.300
Before we move on,
I should mention

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:37.600
that after you've
defined this function,

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:39.890
the SDK gives you
simple operations where

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:42.800
you can compile this
higher level workflow

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:46.580
definition into a tar.gz
file that can be uploaded

00:18:46.580 --> 00:18:47.750
to the pipelines back end.

00:18:47.750 --> 00:18:50.390
Or, you can also just
use our client API

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:53.690
to submit the pipeline to the
Kubeflow Pipelines back end.

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:56.000
And once it's uploaded
to the pipeline system,

00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:00.150
any user can come in, pick
that pipeline, and execute it.

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:04.700
Actually, let me
depict that over here.

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:07.420
So let's say you go in.

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:12.370
You want to create a new
run, so you create a new run

00:19:12.370 --> 00:19:16.940
by specifying a pipeline.

00:19:16.940 --> 00:19:19.970
And then as soon as you specify
a pipeline, you give it a name.

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:26.220
And then, you actually
have this form

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:30.540
that is automatically populated,
which specifies the inputs

00:19:30.540 --> 00:19:32.770
that you need to provide
to this pipeline.

00:19:32.770 --> 00:19:35.220
And then this is where the
annotations to the function

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:36.810
arguments were important.

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:40.350
So the annotations specify
the names of each input,

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:42.960
and the annotations also
specify the default values

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:43.920
for these inputs.

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:49.080
So all you had to do was specify
the pipeline as a function,

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:51.470
specify your function
arguments and annotate them,

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:54.540
and that automatically becomes
this easy to consume form

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:57.770
that someone can use when they
want to execute the pipeline.

00:19:57.770 --> 00:19:59.580
And this is important
because often,

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:01.590
the author of the
pipeline is not

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:03.549
going to be the one that
executes the pipeline.

00:20:03.549 --> 00:20:06.006
In many cases, the author is
going to execute the pipeline,

00:20:06.006 --> 00:20:07.590
but we want to get
to a world where

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:11.640
the author of the pipeline
can give a pre-built pipeline

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:13.210
to others to go and execute.

00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:16.470
So I can come in and not
know too many details

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:18.870
about how a particular
pipeline works under the hood.

00:20:18.870 --> 00:20:21.660
But I can come in and see
what inputs are required,

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:23.850
type them into this form,
and execute the pipeline.

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:26.370
It's as easy as that.

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:29.260
All right, going back
to the notebook here.

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:32.820
Now, let's talk about how the
different steps of the pipeline

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:34.172
are specified.

00:20:34.172 --> 00:20:35.880
As I mentioned, each
step of the pipeline

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:37.150
is a containerized step.

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:41.850
So in its most basic
and most flexible form,

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:44.500
all you have to do is--

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:46.500
so let's take this
example here where

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:48.420
I've built a container
that encapsulates

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:50.100
one step of my pipeline.

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:53.040
I can take this
container, and I basically

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:55.740
wrap it with a
Python function where

00:20:55.740 --> 00:20:58.971
I invoke this operation
called the ContainerOp.

00:20:58.971 --> 00:21:01.470
So this ContainerOp, which is
part of the Kubeflow Pipelines

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:03.930
SDK, takes as
input the container

00:21:03.930 --> 00:21:06.000
that encapsulates the step.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:10.320
And also, it takes as input
the various parameters

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:12.930
that need to be passed
in by the end user.

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:17.760
And this ContainerOp takes
care of invoking that container

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:20.200
and passing it with
corresponding parameters.

00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:24.030
Now often, each container has
its own default entry point.

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:27.182
And often by default, that's
the entry point that's executed.

00:21:27.182 --> 00:21:29.640
But sometimes, containers can
have multiple different entry

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:31.223
points, and you can
override the entry

00:21:31.223 --> 00:21:36.120
point that needs to be
invoked by using a command.

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:37.630
It's called command.

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:39.900
You can specify that
inside the ContainerOp

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.480
and then specify
which entry point has

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:43.989
to be used for a container.

00:21:43.989 --> 00:21:46.530
So long story short, once you
have encapsulated your pipeline

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:48.060
as a container,
all you have to do

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:50.640
is wrap it with
this ContainerOp.

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:56.130
And this produces, essentially,
a Python function version

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:59.010
of this operation that
you can then use when

00:21:59.010 --> 00:22:00.937
you're defining your workflow.

00:22:00.937 --> 00:22:03.270
Now at this point, you're
wondering, hey, wait a minute.

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:05.430
Does that mean every
step that I author,

00:22:05.430 --> 00:22:09.420
every step of the pipeline needs
to be built as a container?

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:10.510
That is not the case.

00:22:10.510 --> 00:22:14.310
I actually wanted to start
with the most flexible method

00:22:14.310 --> 00:22:16.957
of defining pipeline
steps, because anything

00:22:16.957 --> 00:22:18.790
that can be put in a
container is fair game.

00:22:18.790 --> 00:22:21.550
It can be C++ code, R
scripts, and what have you.

00:22:21.550 --> 00:22:24.000
But often, you will
not, as an end user,

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:25.980
have to build the
containers yourself.

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:30.090
You can actually go in and
just author steps of a pipeline

00:22:30.090 --> 00:22:32.350
as a simple Python function.

00:22:32.350 --> 00:22:34.370
And then after you've
authored the function,

00:22:34.370 --> 00:22:38.400
annotate that function with
specific function decorators

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:40.500
that help encapsulate
that function

00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:42.060
as a step of the pipeline.

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:46.320
And when you specify
the function decorators,

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:50.520
all you have to do is
specify a base Docker

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:55.080
image that is used to package
that step of the pipeline.

00:22:55.080 --> 00:22:59.161
Often, you can get base Docker
images by going to Docker Hub.

00:22:59.161 --> 00:23:01.410
For example, if you're
training a TensorFlow pipeline,

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:04.890
the TensorFlow community
provides a bunch

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:06.930
of pre-built Docker
containers that

00:23:06.930 --> 00:23:09.960
are available on Docker Hub with
various versions of TensorFlow

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:11.430
and common utilities.

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:13.890
And you can just simply
reuse those containers.

00:23:13.890 --> 00:23:15.820
Or if you're working in
a large organization,

00:23:15.820 --> 00:23:18.720
you may have your
internal Docker Hub.

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:21.420
But in many cases, a
pre-built container

00:23:21.420 --> 00:23:23.640
may not have the
various libraries

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:25.980
that you specifically need.

00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:28.180
And again, we have you
covered over there.

00:23:28.180 --> 00:23:32.460
So all you have to do is
use this notebook magic

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:36.270
that we have provided, where
you specify the base container

00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:37.180
that you want to use.

00:23:37.180 --> 00:23:39.763
So in this particular case, I'm
going to use a TensorFlow base

00:23:39.763 --> 00:23:40.530
container.

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:44.250
And then after that, simply PIP
install the other dependencies

00:23:44.250 --> 00:23:45.150
that you need.

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:48.390
And of course, folks
that use Python are very

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:49.600
comfortable with PIP install.

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:51.558
So you don't need to know
the details of Docker

00:23:51.558 --> 00:23:52.500
or Dockerization.

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:54.480
All you have to do is
use this notebook magic,

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:56.757
specify a base container,
and then specify

00:23:56.757 --> 00:23:59.340
the other dependencies that need
to be packaged into that base

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:00.090
container.

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:02.460
And we automatically
produce a container

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:05.070
that you can use as the
step of the pipeline,

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:07.780
and it'll have your
custom dependencies.

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:13.505
So long story short, you don't
have to be an expert in Docker

00:24:13.505 --> 00:24:15.930
to author each individual
step of the pipeline.

00:24:15.930 --> 00:24:17.730
We give you the
utilities that you

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:23.910
need to simplify the authoring
of the various steps.

00:24:23.910 --> 00:24:27.000
All right, so that was authoring
of machine learning pipelines.

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:31.080
Now let's go back and look at
the other key value proposition

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:34.080
of Kubeflow Pipelines,
which is rapid, reliable

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:35.670
experimentation.

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:39.960
And this is important because,
particularly in applied machine

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:42.780
learning, when you're building
machine learning applications,

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:46.080
it's all about trying
many different techniques.

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:48.150
The more techniques
you can try, the higher

00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:49.840
your likelihood of success.

00:24:49.840 --> 00:24:51.600
And that is what
we want to enable.

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:54.900
We want to make it very easy
for you to simply author

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:58.190
an initial pipeline,
clone that pipeline,

00:24:58.190 --> 00:25:00.450
edit it to try some
variant of that pipeline,

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:01.440
and then submit it.

00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:03.520
And keep doing this
loop very quickly

00:25:03.520 --> 00:25:05.640
so you can try multiple
different variants.

00:25:05.640 --> 00:25:08.530
And of course, for each pipeline
that you have submitted,

00:25:08.530 --> 00:25:12.170
you can view those pipelines
in the Kubeflow Pipelines

00:25:12.170 --> 00:25:12.670
consoles.

00:25:12.670 --> 00:25:14.700
So you could have
thousands of executions.

00:25:14.700 --> 00:25:17.850
You can go in, and you
can see rich information

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:19.950
about each execution.

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:22.180
What is particularly
compelling is, as I mentioned,

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:25.350
we want to make it easy
for you to try variants

00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:28.274
of your pipeline by
simply cloning it, editing

00:25:28.274 --> 00:25:29.190
it, and submitting it.

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:30.620
And let me actually demo that.

00:25:30.620 --> 00:25:32.910
So take an existing pipeline.

00:25:32.910 --> 00:25:34.540
Hit the Clone button.

00:25:34.540 --> 00:25:38.250
And that opens up this web
form that you can now tweak.

00:25:38.250 --> 00:25:40.860
You can update the
parameters that go in.

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:43.720
And then you can submit that
cloned variant of the pipeline.

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:45.580
And you can do this ad nauseam.

00:25:45.580 --> 00:25:47.580
And pretty soon, we'll
actually add capabilities

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:50.670
where you can clone a pipeline,
see a version of the code,

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:52.370
tweak the code, and submit.

00:25:52.370 --> 00:25:55.020
So long story short, try
multiple different variants

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:56.700
to see what works best.

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:58.620
Now for each variant
of your pipeline,

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:00.930
you can actually see
rich metrics produced

00:26:00.930 --> 00:26:02.260
by that pipeline quickly.

00:26:02.260 --> 00:26:04.950
So you can identify the
ones that worked well.

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:09.382
And then you can select, say,
a subset of the pipelines.

00:26:09.382 --> 00:26:11.340
Let's just say you want
to do some comparisons.

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:17.140
Because often, when you've
launched multiple variants

00:26:17.140 --> 00:26:20.500
of a pipeline, you want to
go in and compare them to see

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:22.090
what was different about them.

00:26:22.090 --> 00:26:24.430
So I can select a
subset of pipelines

00:26:24.430 --> 00:26:26.350
and hit the Compare Runs button.

00:26:26.350 --> 00:26:29.092
And I get this
rich UI where I get

00:26:29.092 --> 00:26:31.300
rich information, the metrics,
and config parameters,

00:26:31.300 --> 00:26:34.360
and so forth of those
executions, side by side,

00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:37.510
so I can easily analyze
them and figure out

00:26:37.510 --> 00:26:39.589
what was different
about each execution.

00:26:39.589 --> 00:26:41.380
And what is particularly
compelling-- and I

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:44.230
find this feature very
interesting-- if you've

00:26:44.230 --> 00:26:47.380
done training where you produce
metrics that can be visualized

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:51.100
by TensorBoard for the various
experiments that you selected,

00:26:51.100 --> 00:26:53.950
with one click, you can
open a TensorBoard instance

00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:56.200
where all the metrics from
those different experiments

00:26:56.200 --> 00:26:58.530
are plotted in the
same sort of plot.

00:26:58.530 --> 00:27:03.310
So you can do rich analysis
of your experiments.

00:27:03.310 --> 00:27:07.300
And then the last
piece is reusability

00:27:07.300 --> 00:27:09.310
of pipelines and components.

00:27:09.310 --> 00:27:10.570
So let me quickly depict that.

00:27:10.570 --> 00:27:12.903
So let's just say you've
authored a multi-step pipeline.

00:27:12.903 --> 00:27:15.790
In this particular case, you
have a six step pipeline.

00:27:15.790 --> 00:27:19.360
Not every step of the pipeline
has to be authored by you.

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:22.450
In many cases, you will find
existing components from, say,

00:27:22.450 --> 00:27:26.950
the AI Hub that you can reuse
as a part of your pipeline.

00:27:26.950 --> 00:27:29.140
Another example is where
you might have an existing

00:27:29.140 --> 00:27:31.900
pipeline, and you just want to
swap one step of the pipeline

00:27:31.900 --> 00:27:33.005
with a different step.

00:27:33.005 --> 00:27:34.930
So let's just say you
have a pipeline that

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:37.190
uses scikit-learn
to do some training.

00:27:37.190 --> 00:27:39.790
And then you soon realize
that, in the AI Hub,

00:27:39.790 --> 00:27:42.460
there is a TensorFlow based
implementation that might just

00:27:42.460 --> 00:27:43.940
work great for your case.

00:27:43.940 --> 00:27:46.797
Well fantastic, just simply
swap the training component

00:27:46.797 --> 00:27:47.380
of a pipeline.

00:27:47.380 --> 00:27:49.900
And now you have a new
variant that you can try.

00:27:49.900 --> 00:27:51.700
So that's how Kubeflow
Pipelines helps

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:55.570
you go from prototyping
to production real quick.

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:59.350
And with that, I'd like to
introduce Andreas Rodriguez

00:27:59.350 --> 00:28:02.620
to talk about how you can use
OpenVINO to get the most out

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:04.466
of your Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:28:07.938 --> 00:28:10.914
[NO AUDIO]

00:29:07.458 --> 00:29:08.860
ANDREAS RODRIGUEZ:
Thanks, Anand,

00:29:08.860 --> 00:29:12.310
for introducing the
Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:29:12.310 --> 00:29:14.934
Before I proceed, I
encourage you to continue

00:29:14.934 --> 00:29:15.850
typing your questions.

00:29:15.850 --> 00:29:20.410
And there are Google and Intel
engineers answering them.

00:29:20.410 --> 00:29:24.010
Intel and Google are partnering
to enhance the Kubeflow

00:29:24.010 --> 00:29:25.000
Pipelines.

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:29.710
As a first step, Intel added the
OpenVINO toolkit as containers

00:29:29.710 --> 00:29:32.120
to Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:29:32.120 --> 00:29:35.970
To start, I want to motivate
the reason for the OpenVINO

00:29:35.970 --> 00:29:40.110
toolkit, then give a brief
overview of OpenVINO,

00:29:40.110 --> 00:29:43.520
and then share how OpenVINO
can be used with the Kubeflow

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:45.200
Pipelines.

00:29:45.200 --> 00:29:47.450
Intel provides a
diversity of hardware

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:50.390
that can be used for
artificial intelligence.

00:29:50.390 --> 00:29:52.460
Each one has
different strengths,

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:55.850
such as better throughput,
better latency, or better

00:29:55.850 --> 00:29:59.060
power, or better
memory, or better cost,

00:29:59.060 --> 00:30:04.150
or better generality, depending
on the specific application.

00:30:04.150 --> 00:30:07.510
Intel's AI hardware products
includes the various types

00:30:07.510 --> 00:30:13.510
of CPUs for both data center
and client, GPUs, VPUs

00:30:13.510 --> 00:30:18.460
or visual processing
units, FPGAs, and NNPs

00:30:18.460 --> 00:30:23.020
or neural network
processor accelerators.

00:30:23.020 --> 00:30:25.540
Given the hardware
diversity, Intel

00:30:25.540 --> 00:30:29.830
develop a unified API
and software toolkit

00:30:29.830 --> 00:30:33.430
that can be used for inference
across all the various hardware

00:30:33.430 --> 00:30:35.280
products.

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:39.210
This software tool
is known as OpenVINO,

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:42.390
which stands for
open visual inference

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:44.910
and neural net optimization.

00:30:44.910 --> 00:30:48.500
OpenVINO has three main
components, the deep learning

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:54.380
deployment tool kit or DLDT,
an OpenCV and Media SDK

00:30:54.380 --> 00:30:58.220
used for traditional vision
and video processing,

00:30:58.220 --> 00:31:02.430
and a collection of pre
train models and tutorials.

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:06.300
At a high level, OpenVINO
provides a unified programming

00:31:06.300 --> 00:31:11.340
model across various hardware,
is optimized for performance

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:15.870
and memory footprint,
is extendable for custom

00:31:15.870 --> 00:31:20.770
workloads, provides broad
operating system support,

00:31:20.770 --> 00:31:25.380
is self-contained for a simple,
out-of-the-box experience,

00:31:25.380 --> 00:31:28.740
and is open source for
the community to enhance

00:31:28.740 --> 00:31:30.750
and contribute.

00:31:30.750 --> 00:31:33.690
The main component that we
will focus in this presentation

00:31:33.690 --> 00:31:37.170
is DLDT, Intel's deep
learning deployment tool kit.

00:31:37.170 --> 00:31:40.470
It offers a model optimizer
and an inference engine

00:31:40.470 --> 00:31:44.160
for optimal inference across
various Intel hardware

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:45.590
products.

00:31:45.590 --> 00:31:48.560
The deep learning deployment
tool kit, or DLDT,

00:31:48.560 --> 00:31:51.470
is easy to use and
provides high performance

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:53.480
across Intel's
hardware products.

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:57.260
Before using DLDT, you
first need to train a model.

00:31:57.260 --> 00:31:58.940
You can train a
model using one of

00:31:58.940 --> 00:32:00.920
the many popular deep
learning frameworks

00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:02.440
on your hardware of choice.

00:32:02.440 --> 00:32:05.720
If you're training on
Intel Xeon processors,

00:32:05.720 --> 00:32:10.370
use the Intel MKL-DNN library
to accelerate your training.

00:32:10.370 --> 00:32:12.510
DLDT has two steps.

00:32:12.510 --> 00:32:16.010
First, DLDT provides an
optimized intermediate

00:32:16.010 --> 00:32:21.020
representation, or IR,
over the model's graph.

00:32:21.020 --> 00:32:25.010
This includes optimization,
such as layer fusions

00:32:25.010 --> 00:32:26.900
or [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:31.900
Second, this IR is then consumed
by the inference engine,

00:32:31.900 --> 00:32:35.360
optimized across various
hardware products.

00:32:35.360 --> 00:32:38.540
On the left in this
slide, you can see a list

00:32:38.540 --> 00:32:41.030
of currently supported layers.

00:32:41.030 --> 00:32:45.680
Intel continues to
expand this list.

00:32:45.680 --> 00:32:48.680
If a given layer is not
currently supported,

00:32:48.680 --> 00:32:51.170
documentation is
provided detailing

00:32:51.170 --> 00:32:54.530
how users can add new
parameters or layers,

00:32:54.530 --> 00:32:58.640
or how to further
optimize existing layers.

00:32:58.640 --> 00:33:01.730
A feature of DLDT is
heterogeneous execution,

00:33:01.730 --> 00:33:05.330
or the ability to execute a
model across various hardware

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:06.440
products.

00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:08.450
In this example,
there is a primitive

00:33:08.450 --> 00:33:12.350
of a model not supported
or optimized for FPGA, that

00:33:12.350 --> 00:33:16.480
is crucial to execute on a CPU.

00:33:16.480 --> 00:33:18.700
Another feature is
asynchronous execution

00:33:18.700 --> 00:33:20.650
to hide data transfers.

00:33:20.650 --> 00:33:23.960
That is, as one inference
cycle is executed,

00:33:23.960 --> 00:33:29.330
OpenVINO fetches the data
for the next inference cycle.

00:33:29.330 --> 00:33:32.530
Once you have your optimized
intermediate representation,

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:35.030
using the DLDT
Inference Engine is

00:33:35.030 --> 00:33:37.920
as simple as these
five lines of code.

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:40.250
Note that in the third line
of code that's highlighted,

00:33:40.250 --> 00:33:43.580
the user can select from
multiple hardware products.

00:33:43.580 --> 00:33:46.370
This enables the user to
quickly compare performance

00:33:46.370 --> 00:33:48.930
across various hardware
products to determine

00:33:48.930 --> 00:33:50.750
which one may be
the most suitable

00:33:50.750 --> 00:33:53.370
for a particular model.

00:33:53.370 --> 00:33:55.320
Intel has been working
with several customers

00:33:55.320 --> 00:33:59.100
to enable them to do AI on
Intel's hardware products.

00:33:59.100 --> 00:34:02.610
Here are a few examples
of customers we work with

00:34:02.610 --> 00:34:04.290
and their testimonials.

00:34:04.290 --> 00:34:07.620
GE, for example,
observed a 14 times

00:34:07.620 --> 00:34:10.920
performance improvement
using the OpenVINO toolkit

00:34:10.920 --> 00:34:14.909
for their task of inference
across X-ray imagery.

00:34:14.909 --> 00:34:16.800
Several other
consumers report up

00:34:16.800 --> 00:34:20.040
to two orders of magnitude
performance gains

00:34:20.040 --> 00:34:25.517
on Intel Xeon processors,
using Intel's optimizations.

00:34:25.517 --> 00:34:27.850
Intel and Google are partnering
to bring the performance

00:34:27.850 --> 00:34:30.489
and ease of use of
OpenVINO to the masses

00:34:30.489 --> 00:34:34.330
by adding DLDT containers
to the Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:34:34.330 --> 00:34:36.070
Let's review the
Kubeflow Pipeline

00:34:36.070 --> 00:34:38.050
via a hypothetical example.

00:34:38.050 --> 00:34:41.380
Suppose you're a data scientist
at American AI company,

00:34:41.380 --> 00:34:45.250
examining X-rays to
detect cancerous cells.

00:34:45.250 --> 00:34:48.310
There are various containers
to facilitate your work.

00:34:48.310 --> 00:34:51.070
The data ingestion,
data transformation,

00:34:51.070 --> 00:34:53.690
and data validation
containers facilitate

00:34:53.690 --> 00:34:57.430
reading data, transforming the
data to the expected format,

00:34:57.430 --> 00:34:59.930
and validating that the
X-ray data is usable

00:34:59.930 --> 00:35:01.890
and properly labeled.

00:35:01.890 --> 00:35:05.560
This data is passed to
the trainer container.

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:08.050
The output in this
hypothetical example

00:35:08.050 --> 00:35:10.300
is a trained cancer
detection model

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:12.910
that is passed through the
model validation container

00:35:12.910 --> 00:35:17.710
to ensure the model is ready
for deployment or serving.

00:35:17.710 --> 00:35:21.100
The train model is then used for
serving by your medical company

00:35:21.100 --> 00:35:23.860
to examine patients' X-rays.

00:35:23.860 --> 00:35:28.270
The DLDT Model Optimizer
takes a train model

00:35:28.270 --> 00:35:36.460
and gets an optimized
intermediate representation,

00:35:36.460 --> 00:35:38.890
which is passed to
the DLDT Inference

00:35:38.890 --> 00:35:43.480
Engine for high performance
across the various hardware

00:35:43.480 --> 00:35:45.130
back ends.

00:35:45.130 --> 00:35:47.740
Note that not all the
train containers are needed

00:35:47.740 --> 00:35:49.760
to use the DLDT containers.

00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:55.280
The DLDT Model Optimizer can
use an existing train model.

00:35:55.280 --> 00:35:58.020
We have only provided
a high level overview.

00:35:58.020 --> 00:36:00.540
We invite you to try
the Kubeflow Pipelines

00:36:00.540 --> 00:36:02.280
with OpenVINO containers.

00:36:02.280 --> 00:36:04.800
In the links provided, we
offer two simple examples

00:36:04.800 --> 00:36:08.820
for each of the DLDT containers
to help you get started.

00:36:08.820 --> 00:36:12.420
And you can learn more about
the benefits of OpenVINO

00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:15.110
using the link at the bottom.

00:36:15.110 --> 00:36:19.620
Stay tuned now for live Q&amp;A.
We will be back in less than a

00:36:19.620 --> 00:36:22.535
minute.

